WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.880
- Hi
(audience applauding)

00:00:00.880 --> 00:00:02.290
- I like that.

00:00:02.290 --> 00:00:04.340
This is so fun.
- I'm gonna tell my stylist

00:00:04.340 --> 00:00:05.173
to get me that.

00:00:05.173 --> 00:00:06.006
That's really cute.

00:00:06.006 --> 00:00:07.490
How have you been?
- I'm great. I'm great.

00:00:07.490 --> 00:00:08.830
- Yeah?
- By the way,

00:00:08.830 --> 00:00:10.490
congratulations on the show-

00:00:10.490 --> 00:00:12.003
- Thank you-
- And all your success.

00:00:12.003 --> 00:00:13.437
- It's so random.

00:00:13.437 --> 00:00:14.990
And it was funny cause you were coming

00:00:14.990 --> 00:00:16.710
and I was like, how full
circle moment is this?

00:00:16.710 --> 00:00:18.140
'Cause like, right after I won "Idol,"

00:00:18.140 --> 00:00:19.410
they rushed us to New York,

00:00:19.410 --> 00:00:21.600
and y'all, you were one of
my first interviews ever.

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:23.210
- That's right.
- So I feel intimidated,

00:00:23.210 --> 00:00:24.337
but I'm gonna be fine.
- No, don't be intimidated.

00:00:24.337 --> 00:00:26.590
It was so funny because I remember,

00:00:26.590 --> 00:00:27.780
you were super hoarse,

00:00:27.780 --> 00:00:31.540
you were on this whirlwind
24 hour press thing,

00:00:31.540 --> 00:00:32.560
'cause you had just won.

00:00:32.560 --> 00:00:33.720
It was so exciting.

00:00:33.720 --> 00:00:34.807
And I remember saying,

00:00:34.807 --> 00:00:38.610
"I can't wait to see what
happens to you in the future."

00:00:38.610 --> 00:00:39.850
- Boy, me neither.
- Look at her.

00:00:39.850 --> 00:00:41.270
- No. Yeah.
(audience cheering)

00:00:41.270 --> 00:00:43.027
Nobody knew, including me.

00:00:43.027 --> 00:00:45.320
- Who knew?
- Yeah, no, definitely not me.

00:00:45.320 --> 00:00:46.517
I was like "Well I should
be able to pay my bills

00:00:46.517 --> 00:00:48.345
for a minute.
(Katie laughing)

00:00:48.345 --> 00:00:50.057
- So, what was your most,

00:00:50.057 --> 00:00:51.170
'cause this is the thing.

00:00:51.170 --> 00:00:52.080
This show isn't live.

00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:53.227
That's a whole different situation

00:00:53.227 --> 00:00:54.130
when you're doing live stuff.

00:00:54.130 --> 00:00:56.640
Like when I host award shows,
it's a different animal.

00:00:56.640 --> 00:00:58.280
So out of the "Today
Show" all those years,

00:00:58.280 --> 00:01:01.600
like what was your most
favorite like unplanned moment?

00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:02.592
- Well, I have a lot of them,

00:01:02.592 --> 00:01:03.425
you know.
- Okay.

00:01:03.425 --> 00:01:05.370
- But I did the show for 15 years.

00:01:05.370 --> 00:01:08.610
I think, well, one time
a woman exposed herself

00:01:08.610 --> 00:01:10.300
when she was out on the Plaza

00:01:10.300 --> 00:01:13.610
and the rest of the anchors-
- Did you throw her beads?

00:01:13.610 --> 00:01:15.370
- No, but I was,
(audience laughing)

00:01:15.370 --> 00:01:17.100
I was on a remote location

00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:18.320
and the rest of the anchors,

00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:19.610
she was behind them.

00:01:19.610 --> 00:01:21.330
So they didn't see anything.

00:01:21.330 --> 00:01:24.540
So basically you see
saw me in the box going.

00:01:24.540 --> 00:01:25.494
(Kelly laughing)

00:01:25.494 --> 00:01:27.680
And so that was great. I
mean, there's been a lot.

00:01:27.680 --> 00:01:30.040
And then I was interviewing Barbara Bush

00:01:30.040 --> 00:01:33.030
at the White House for the
anniversary of the White House

00:01:33.030 --> 00:01:34.510
and we were getting a whole tour

00:01:34.510 --> 00:01:36.260
and suddenly her husband,

00:01:36.260 --> 00:01:38.520
President George Herbert Walker Bush,

00:01:38.520 --> 00:01:40.610
walked in with their dog

00:01:40.610 --> 00:01:43.410
and I had to do an impromptu
19 minute interview

00:01:43.410 --> 00:01:45.030
that I hadn't prepared for-

00:01:45.030 --> 00:01:46.810
- I bet those 19 minutes
- Which was really-

00:01:46.810 --> 00:01:47.699
- Lasted forever.
- Oh my God.

00:01:47.699 --> 00:01:49.353
(everyone laughing)

00:01:49.353 --> 00:01:50.857
I was so pitted out

00:01:50.857 --> 00:01:52.250
and it was so stressful

00:01:52.250 --> 00:01:54.150
and I kept thinking, I'm
gonna run outta questions.

00:01:54.150 --> 00:01:55.267
I'm gonna start asking him,

00:01:55.267 --> 00:01:57.300
"What's your favorite food
here at the White House?"

00:01:57.300 --> 00:01:58.280
- Oh my gosh.
- Or, you know,

00:01:58.280 --> 00:01:59.167
but it all worked out.

00:01:59.167 --> 00:02:00.520
- Oh, that's so cool though.

00:02:00.520 --> 00:02:02.390
That's a really cool
impromptu kind of interview.

00:02:02.390 --> 00:02:03.350
- Yeah,
- Nobody gets

00:02:03.350 --> 00:02:04.290
that chance often.

00:02:04.290 --> 00:02:05.123
- I know
- It's cool.

00:02:05.123 --> 00:02:05.956
- It was funny.

00:02:05.956 --> 00:02:07.480
I can't believe that he decided to walk in

00:02:07.480 --> 00:02:09.820
in the middle of our
interview, but he did,

00:02:09.820 --> 00:02:11.450
and it was great and-
- I love that.

00:02:11.450 --> 00:02:13.440
- That was when I had first
started on the "Today Show"

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:14.273
so-

00:02:14.273 --> 00:02:15.930
- Oh my God.
- It was a big moment for me.

00:02:15.930 --> 00:02:17.170
- It's like, welcome.
- Definitely.

00:02:17.170 --> 00:02:18.540
- Wait, I wanna talk about this too

00:02:18.540 --> 00:02:20.290
because you just turned 65, right?

00:02:20.290 --> 00:02:21.682
Did I hear right that you're 65

00:02:21.682 --> 00:02:23.720
and you're doing 65 acts of kindness.

00:02:23.720 --> 00:02:24.553
- I am.

00:02:24.553 --> 00:02:25.386
- I love that.

00:02:25.386 --> 00:02:28.170
- You know, I don't need
a darn thing, you know?

00:02:28.170 --> 00:02:30.500
- [Kelly] Yeah, no, I get it
- I've been so blessed.

00:02:30.500 --> 00:02:33.740
And I thought, what can I do
that maybe will spread joy

00:02:33.740 --> 00:02:36.240
and maybe encourage other
people to follow suit.

00:02:36.240 --> 00:02:37.880
So I said, I'm 65.

00:02:37.880 --> 00:02:40.880
I'm gonna do, in addition
to signing up for Medicare,

00:02:40.880 --> 00:02:41.870
I'm gonna get 60,

00:02:41.870 --> 00:02:43.900
I'm gonna do 60-
(everyone laughing)

00:02:43.900 --> 00:02:45.060
It's really depressing

00:02:45.060 --> 00:02:46.600
when you get those notifications.

00:02:46.600 --> 00:02:47.651
I mean, getting like my
ARP card was bad enough.

00:02:47.651 --> 00:02:50.477
- Whatever! It's a discount people!

00:02:50.477 --> 00:02:51.310
(Katie laughing)
- Oh, I know!

00:02:51.310 --> 00:02:52.690
- A discount is always awesome.

00:02:52.690 --> 00:02:54.450
- I guess I'm eligible
for the senior discount.

00:02:54.450 --> 00:02:55.283
- I'm just saying.

00:02:55.283 --> 00:02:56.670
I told my mom, my mom got so mad.

00:02:56.670 --> 00:02:58.520
I called her, I don't
know what age it was,

00:02:58.520 --> 00:03:00.097
but it was like, she turned
a certain age, I was like,

00:03:00.097 --> 00:03:02.028
"Girl you can get me some
free food at Denny's."

00:03:02.028 --> 00:03:03.863
I was like, "You can give me like half off

00:03:03.863 --> 00:03:05.690
and like, I'm like, I'm
taking you everywhere."

00:03:05.690 --> 00:03:07.941
I was like, she didn't think it was funny.

00:03:07.941 --> 00:03:08.774
- She didn't? I think it's funny.

00:03:08.774 --> 00:03:10.828
- I think she thinks of
age differently than me.

00:03:10.828 --> 00:03:11.780
(both laughing)

00:03:11.780 --> 00:03:12.613
- But I'm doing,

00:03:12.613 --> 00:03:15.180
you know, I'm trying to
just do things every day

00:03:15.180 --> 00:03:16.643
that are acts of kindness, you know?

00:03:16.643 --> 00:03:17.590
For someone.
- I think it's a good thing,

00:03:17.590 --> 00:03:18.470
though, to put in your brain, though,

00:03:18.470 --> 00:03:19.440
'cause it makes you start,

00:03:19.440 --> 00:03:20.740
like, I know you're doing 65

00:03:20.740 --> 00:03:22.490
but it'll probably get you in the habit

00:03:22.490 --> 00:03:23.323
of everyday kind of thinking like that,

00:03:23.323 --> 00:03:24.980
which is what we all should do.

00:03:24.980 --> 00:03:25.813
- Definitely.

00:03:25.813 --> 00:03:27.890
And I want everyone to
kind of follow suit, so.

00:03:27.890 --> 00:03:28.723
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:03:28.723 --> 00:03:30.527
- And you can do your age.
- The world needs it.

00:03:30.527 --> 00:03:32.830
- Or you can do more than your age, right?

00:03:32.830 --> 00:03:34.440
- I'm gonna be 40 this year. I'll do it.

00:03:34.440 --> 00:03:35.449
I'll do it just for today.
- Yeah!

00:03:35.449 --> 00:03:36.282
- Yeah.
- That would be fun.

00:03:36.282 --> 00:03:37.115
- I know.
- You should do it

00:03:37.115 --> 00:03:37.948
on the show.

00:03:37.948 --> 00:03:39.110
- I will do it on the,

00:03:39.110 --> 00:03:40.139
she has just made it so.

00:03:40.139 --> 00:03:42.510
(audience applauding)
She has just made it so.

00:03:42.510 --> 00:03:43.343
We will do something

00:03:43.343 --> 00:03:44.176
with the show.
- I'm now producing

00:03:44.176 --> 00:03:45.093
"The Kelly Clarkson Show."

00:03:45.093 --> 00:03:46.220
- It's a good idea though.

00:03:46.220 --> 00:03:47.490
We love giving people stuff here.

00:03:47.490 --> 00:03:48.750
But also, wait, speaking of that.

00:03:48.750 --> 00:03:51.470
So March is, I wanna say this
right, colorectal, right?

00:03:51.470 --> 00:03:52.303
- Yes, colorectal.
- I always feel like

00:03:52.303 --> 00:03:53.150
I'm saying it wrong.

00:03:53.150 --> 00:03:54.050
Okay.
- Yeah, I know.

00:03:54.050 --> 00:03:55.990
- I know, everybody says it differently.

00:03:55.990 --> 00:03:57.924
Okay, so March is Colorectal
Cancer Awareness Month

00:03:57.924 --> 00:04:00.120
and I know that you're a big advocate

00:04:00.120 --> 00:04:01.305
of people getting screened, right?

00:04:01.305 --> 00:04:02.138
- Yes.

00:04:02.138 --> 00:04:04.340
- I've actually, polyps run in our family,

00:04:04.340 --> 00:04:05.520
so I've already done it

00:04:05.520 --> 00:04:07.660
and I'm only 39, but like-
- Well I'm glad 'cause-

00:04:07.660 --> 00:04:08.820
- People should get checked out.

00:04:08.820 --> 00:04:09.750
- Yeah, definitely.

00:04:09.750 --> 00:04:10.960
This is so important.

00:04:10.960 --> 00:04:13.320
Let me use this as a sounding board

00:04:13.320 --> 00:04:17.660
because, you know, it's so
many people don't get screened

00:04:17.660 --> 00:04:20.680
and the American Cancer Society
lowered the screening age

00:04:20.680 --> 00:04:21.513
to 45.

00:04:21.513 --> 00:04:22.346
- Yeah.

00:04:22.346 --> 00:04:23.890
- Because we're seeing many more cases

00:04:23.890 --> 00:04:27.250
of colorectal cancer and
people under the age of 50.

00:04:27.250 --> 00:04:29.810
- So I've teamed up with Exact Sciences.

00:04:29.810 --> 00:04:31.360
They're the maker of Cologuard

00:04:31.360 --> 00:04:35.240
because while I got a
colonoscopy on the "Today Show"

00:04:35.240 --> 00:04:37.770
to spread awareness after Jay died,

00:04:37.770 --> 00:04:40.010
there a lot of different screening options

00:04:40.010 --> 00:04:42.327
that are available.
- Yeah.

00:04:42.327 --> 00:04:45.120
- And so I just want people to know

00:04:45.120 --> 00:04:46.970
that it's highly preventable,

00:04:46.970 --> 00:04:48.740
but the least prevented cancer.

00:04:48.740 --> 00:04:51.390
There are more than 40 million
people at average risk.

00:04:51.390 --> 00:04:53.520
Not like you who have polyps

00:04:53.520 --> 00:04:55.380
or somebody who has a family history.

00:04:55.380 --> 00:04:57.010
- Yeah.
- Who aren't getting screened

00:04:57.010 --> 00:04:59.770
and it can be really
stopped in it's tracks

00:04:59.770 --> 00:05:02.230
if you detect it or early.
- Yeah.

00:05:02.230 --> 00:05:03.620
- When it's most treatable

00:05:03.620 --> 00:05:06.560
or, you know, it's just, you
can breathe a sigh of relief.

00:05:06.560 --> 00:05:08.770
So I really want everyone-
- And why not just try?

00:05:08.770 --> 00:05:12.300
- If they they're 45 or if
they know someone who's 45

00:05:12.300 --> 00:05:14.140
and they haven't been screened

00:05:14.140 --> 00:05:17.190
to talk to their doctor about
different screening options,

00:05:17.190 --> 00:05:19.400
including ones that don't require prep

00:05:19.400 --> 00:05:21.100
and don't make you miss a day

00:05:21.100 --> 00:05:22.150
of work.
- Yeah.

00:05:22.150 --> 00:05:24.330
- But it's super, super important.

00:05:24.330 --> 00:05:25.163
'Cause I don't want-
- And it's worth it.

00:05:25.163 --> 00:05:26.790
- People to be saying, "If only."

00:05:26.790 --> 00:05:28.930
- Exactly, if I would've
caught it, that's my thing.

00:05:28.930 --> 00:05:31.330
And that's, with me, I had
no issues, no complications

00:05:31.330 --> 00:05:33.650
but my family, I knew
had polyps in the past

00:05:33.650 --> 00:05:34.890
like a lot of people-
- You're lucky.

00:05:34.890 --> 00:05:35.820
- So that's why I did it.

00:05:35.820 --> 00:05:37.100
I was like, and then I was fine

00:05:37.100 --> 00:05:38.776
but isn't that worth it?

00:05:38.776 --> 00:05:40.384
Like knowing that you're fine.
- Yes!

00:05:40.384 --> 00:05:41.240
- Like, you know what I'm saying?

00:05:41.240 --> 00:05:43.140
- Or if you're not fine-
- That's even better.

00:05:43.140 --> 00:05:45.620
- The earlier you detect it-
- The better chance.

00:05:45.620 --> 00:05:47.037
- Through screening, the better chance

00:05:47.037 --> 00:05:48.502
that you'll continue to be fine.

00:05:48.502 --> 00:05:49.370
- Yeah.

00:05:49.370 --> 00:05:52.524
- Just get screened, everybody, please.

00:05:52.524 --> 00:05:53.680
(audience applauding)

00:05:53.680 --> 00:05:54.800
- And it's not a big deal.

00:05:54.800 --> 00:05:56.840
I think everybody made it a big deal.

00:05:56.840 --> 00:05:57.673
I was freaked out going in

00:05:57.673 --> 00:05:59.160
'cause I was like, "Wait,
what are they gonna do?"

00:05:59.160 --> 00:06:00.030
And then it's like not a big deal.

00:06:00.030 --> 00:06:01.030
- Yeah.
- Yeah.

00:06:01.030 --> 00:06:01.900
- Anyway.
- Yeah.

00:06:01.900 --> 00:06:03.290
- It's really, really important

00:06:03.290 --> 00:06:04.200
and I'm happy

00:06:04.200 --> 00:06:07.910
because I have such a personal
connection to the disease.

00:06:07.910 --> 00:06:09.800
I know what it can do to someone.

00:06:09.800 --> 00:06:11.330
And that's why I've become-
- And to the family.

00:06:11.330 --> 00:06:13.090
It's hard to watch, yeah.
- Oh, yeah, of course.

00:06:13.090 --> 00:06:14.979
I mean, Ellie and Carrie grew up

00:06:14.979 --> 00:06:17.300
without their wonderful father

00:06:17.300 --> 00:06:20.610
and without my incredible husband. And so-

00:06:20.610 --> 00:06:21.648
- I love that you talk about
that though, in your book.

00:06:21.648 --> 00:06:25.310
Being single, you know,
and crushing it still.

00:06:25.310 --> 00:06:27.093
'Cause I think it's a
hard thing, women always,

00:06:28.155 --> 00:06:29.160
and I felt this,

00:06:29.160 --> 00:06:30.880
like maybe a few years ago.

00:06:30.880 --> 00:06:32.990
I was like, "Ooh, how do you balance?"

00:06:32.990 --> 00:06:33.910
I really love my job.

00:06:33.910 --> 00:06:34.743
I love what I do.

00:06:34.743 --> 00:06:35.576
I love that I get to help people.

00:06:35.576 --> 00:06:36.972
But you know, I'm a single mom now

00:06:36.972 --> 00:06:38.781
and it's hard to find that balance

00:06:38.781 --> 00:06:40.440
and you wanna be there for everything.

00:06:40.440 --> 00:06:41.273
But-

00:06:41.273 --> 00:06:42.620
- I think the time you're with them,

00:06:42.620 --> 00:06:44.030
you have to really be focused

00:06:44.030 --> 00:06:45.380
and make sure it's quality time.

00:06:45.380 --> 00:06:48.870
Like don't be on your phone,
really be focused on them.

00:06:48.870 --> 00:06:52.613
And I think for them to see
you going out in the world,

00:06:52.613 --> 00:06:56.803
doing great things,
bringing people happiness.

00:06:56.803 --> 00:06:57.636
- Yeah.

00:06:57.636 --> 00:06:59.710
- You know, putting great
things into the world.

00:06:59.710 --> 00:07:01.110
I remember when Ellie was nine,

00:07:01.110 --> 00:07:03.480
she looked at me in the kitchen,

00:07:03.480 --> 00:07:04.710
We were like setting the table,

00:07:04.710 --> 00:07:05.543
and she said,

00:07:05.543 --> 00:07:07.610
"Mom, I'm so proud of
the work you're doing

00:07:07.610 --> 00:07:09.230
with colon cancer."
- Oh wow.

00:07:09.230 --> 00:07:13.000
- So they watch you and I
think they learn from you.

00:07:13.000 --> 00:07:14.670
And I think they appreciate things

00:07:14.670 --> 00:07:17.740
that you're doing outside the home too.

00:07:17.740 --> 00:07:19.400
So don't feel guilty

00:07:19.400 --> 00:07:21.020
- In honor of Colorectal Cancer Month,

00:07:21.020 --> 00:07:23.100
and your 65 acts of kindness this year,

00:07:23.100 --> 00:07:25.842
our show is gonna donate
$5,000 to Stand Up To Cancer.

00:07:25.842 --> 00:07:26.675
- Oh!
(audience cheering)

00:07:26.675 --> 00:07:28.485
And hopefully everybody out there,

00:07:28.485 --> 00:07:29.650
y'all can pitch in as well.

00:07:29.650 --> 00:07:32.960
Her new book is called "Going There."

00:07:32.960 --> 00:07:35.820
In the book, you talk about
many people from your past.

00:07:35.820 --> 00:07:37.720
Did it feel good to get things out there?

00:07:37.720 --> 00:07:40.440
I feel terrified to do
something like this.

00:07:40.440 --> 00:07:41.273
- Really?
- Yeah.

00:07:41.273 --> 00:07:42.106
- Well, you know,

00:07:42.106 --> 00:07:44.117
as my husband, John said,

00:07:44.117 --> 00:07:45.887
"If you're gonna write a
book, you have to be honest."

00:07:45.887 --> 00:07:47.390
- Exactly.
- And I sort of thought,

00:07:47.390 --> 00:07:50.480
what is the point of, kind,
of sugar coating everything?

00:07:50.480 --> 00:07:52.260
So I'm very honest about,

00:07:52.260 --> 00:07:54.670
and introspective about
mistakes I've made,

00:07:54.670 --> 00:07:57.860
shortcomings, failures,
triumphs, tragedies.

00:07:57.860 --> 00:08:01.730
Sort of, the whole gamut of
what makes up a full life.

00:08:01.730 --> 00:08:04.240
And so, you know, I think
when you're a public figure,

00:08:04.240 --> 00:08:06.380
I'm sure you could relate to this, Kelly,

00:08:06.380 --> 00:08:08.690
what happens is everyone writes about you

00:08:08.690 --> 00:08:11.840
but you never really
get to tell your story.

00:08:11.840 --> 00:08:14.770
And people who write about
you often have an agenda,

00:08:14.770 --> 00:08:17.530
or they're doing it for
clicks, or whatever reason.

00:08:17.530 --> 00:08:20.850
So I was able to really do, you know,

00:08:20.850 --> 00:08:22.380
talk about what it felt like

00:08:22.380 --> 00:08:24.280
from this vantage point.
- Yeah.

00:08:24.280 --> 00:08:26.220
- And plus, I love to write.

00:08:26.220 --> 00:08:27.940
It's sort of funny.

00:08:27.940 --> 00:08:28.773
The book is.

00:08:28.773 --> 00:08:32.080
There are places where it's
really heartbreaking and sad,

00:08:32.080 --> 00:08:33.500
but I did it for my daughters

00:08:33.500 --> 00:08:36.140
because I feel like we all
have these stories to tell,

00:08:36.140 --> 00:08:39.130
and these circumstances that shaped us.

00:08:39.130 --> 00:08:39.972
And we used to kind of,

00:08:39.972 --> 00:08:44.400
I have so many letters
from my great grandmother,

00:08:44.400 --> 00:08:46.360
and my mom saved everything,

00:08:46.360 --> 00:08:48.290
my dad was a saver too.

00:08:48.290 --> 00:08:50.690
But because we don't
write letters anymore,

00:08:50.690 --> 00:08:53.320
some of our history is evaporating.

00:08:53.320 --> 00:08:57.010
So I urge everybody to write things down,

00:08:57.010 --> 00:08:59.680
even if, you know, even if
they don't get it published

00:08:59.680 --> 00:09:03.530
because people want to
have their family history.

00:09:03.530 --> 00:09:05.240
And if your kids aren't interested now,

00:09:05.240 --> 00:09:09.120
they will one day really
wanna know about my mother

00:09:09.120 --> 00:09:11.040
and my father and my grandparents.

00:09:11.040 --> 00:09:14.320
And after all this, you
know, I love my book.

00:09:14.320 --> 00:09:15.153
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:09:15.153 --> 00:09:16.490
- It's my favorite book.
- I love it!

00:09:16.490 --> 00:09:18.530
(audience applauding)

00:09:18.530 --> 00:09:19.813
That's amazing!

00:09:19.813 --> 00:09:22.110
My book is my favorite book.

00:09:22.110 --> 00:09:24.070
You also talk about your current marriage.

00:09:24.070 --> 00:09:27.247
So what was dating like, I
don't know, in your 50's?

00:09:27.247 --> 00:09:28.570
That's a different age

00:09:28.570 --> 00:09:29.940
for some people.
- Yeah.

00:09:29.940 --> 00:09:32.040
So I was 41 when Jay died.

00:09:32.040 --> 00:09:34.710
And so, you know, after a couple years,

00:09:34.710 --> 00:09:38.180
I realized I needed to dip my
toe back into the dating pool

00:09:38.180 --> 00:09:41.250
'cause I'm also a real partner person.

00:09:41.250 --> 00:09:43.330
- Yeah.
- I'm not good alone.

00:09:43.330 --> 00:09:45.720
And I like to have companionship.

00:09:45.720 --> 00:09:47.190
- Yeah.
- So I started dating.

00:09:47.190 --> 00:09:48.171
I had some, you know,

00:09:48.171 --> 00:09:51.100
relationships that didn't
necessarily pan out

00:09:51.100 --> 00:09:52.416
which you can read about in my book.

00:09:52.416 --> 00:09:55.200
(everyone laughing)

00:09:55.200 --> 00:09:58.790
Including with a guy 17
years younger than I was.

00:09:58.790 --> 00:09:59.750
- Oh really?

00:09:59.750 --> 00:10:01.160
I have a girlfriend like you.

00:10:01.160 --> 00:10:02.340
See-
- Now what do you mean,

00:10:02.340 --> 00:10:03.330
you have a girlfriend like me?

00:10:03.330 --> 00:10:04.380
This was one time.

00:10:04.380 --> 00:10:05.710
- I'm just saying, I have
a girlfriend like you.

00:10:05.710 --> 00:10:07.100
She's attracted to younger guys.

00:10:07.100 --> 00:10:08.810
I'm like, I don't wanna be a teacher.

00:10:08.810 --> 00:10:10.380
Like I'm not here to be a teacher.

00:10:10.380 --> 00:10:13.060
- Oh, what did they say?
I'd rather be a nurse.

00:10:13.060 --> 00:10:13.940
Wait, what do they say?

00:10:13.940 --> 00:10:16.115
I'd rather be a babysitter than a nurse.

00:10:16.115 --> 00:10:16.948
- No, no, no.
(Katie laughing)

00:10:16.948 --> 00:10:18.370
I don't wanna be either one.

00:10:18.370 --> 00:10:19.223
(both laughing)
I don't wanna be either one.

00:10:21.040 --> 00:10:22.608
- But anyway, so, but I did,

00:10:22.608 --> 00:10:25.050
I've been married for seven years now.

00:10:25.050 --> 00:10:27.200
I met an incredible person.
- That's so cool.

00:10:27.200 --> 00:10:28.840
- I could not be happier and-

00:10:28.840 --> 00:10:29.740
(audience sighing)

00:10:29.740 --> 00:10:31.673
There he is!
- So great.

00:10:31.673 --> 00:10:32.820
- He's so great

00:10:32.820 --> 00:10:35.100
and he puts up with me.

00:10:35.100 --> 00:10:36.110
I'm a total mess.

00:10:36.110 --> 00:10:37.480
He's super neat.

00:10:37.480 --> 00:10:39.150
And he calls me tornado and-

00:10:39.150 --> 00:10:39.983
- Oh, you're actually messy like-

00:10:39.983 --> 00:10:42.490
- I'm messy. I'm messy.
- Oh my gosh. I'm opposite.

00:10:42.490 --> 00:10:44.950
- In fact, I think he posted on Instagram,

00:10:44.950 --> 00:10:46.813
a shot of my toothpaste tubes

00:10:46.813 --> 00:10:49.060
that was squeezed in the middle.

00:10:49.060 --> 00:10:49.910
Oh.

00:10:49.910 --> 00:10:53.347
And then he said, why does
she have seven toothbrushes?

00:10:53.347 --> 00:10:54.180
(audience laughing)

00:10:54.180 --> 00:10:55.013
And that's an excellent question-

00:10:55.013 --> 00:10:56.677
- Why do you have?

00:10:56.677 --> 00:10:58.560
And you might be asking the same question.

00:10:58.560 --> 00:10:59.760
It's 'cause I travel

00:10:59.760 --> 00:11:01.139
and I always forget it.
- Oh, the hotel?

00:11:01.139 --> 00:11:02.620
- Then I get one from the hotel

00:11:02.620 --> 00:11:04.410
or I go to a local drug store

00:11:04.410 --> 00:11:06.317
and then I have a
perfectly good toothbrush-

00:11:06.317 --> 00:11:07.437
- And you feel wasteful.
- I don't wanna throw it away.

00:11:07.437 --> 00:11:08.270
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:11:08.270 --> 00:11:09.840
- So I just add it to my collection.

00:11:09.840 --> 00:11:12.050
- Yeah. Some people
call that hoarder, but-

00:11:12.050 --> 00:11:12.940
(everyone laughing)

00:11:12.940 --> 00:11:15.066
- I do have sort of
hoarder tendencies, Kelly.

00:11:15.066 --> 00:11:16.290
(both laughing)

00:11:16.290 --> 00:11:17.360
- Okay. So wait, I wanna talk about this

00:11:17.360 --> 00:11:18.950
'cause you started your
own media company and-

00:11:18.950 --> 00:11:20.500
- Yes.
- Was the reason behind it

00:11:20.500 --> 00:11:21.440
'cause you wanted,

00:11:21.440 --> 00:11:22.942
I know a lot of people,
it's confusing right now.

00:11:22.942 --> 00:11:24.710
Like people don't know
who to trust or whatever.

00:11:24.710 --> 00:11:26.900
Is this kind of the
backbone of why you did it?

00:11:26.900 --> 00:11:29.210
- Well, I had worked at
so many different networks

00:11:29.210 --> 00:11:30.870
and in so many different jobs

00:11:30.870 --> 00:11:33.170
and I think in this day and age,

00:11:33.170 --> 00:11:35.100
there's so much content out there,

00:11:35.100 --> 00:11:37.380
so much information, 24/7,

00:11:37.380 --> 00:11:38.840
it's kind of inundated.

00:11:38.840 --> 00:11:40.820
You're inundated with that.
- And people just lie.

00:11:40.820 --> 00:11:41.653
- Yeah. Right.
- So that's hard.

00:11:41.653 --> 00:11:43.780
- And there's a lot of
misinformation out there.

00:11:43.780 --> 00:11:46.760
So I thought, you know, I've
been doing this a long time.

00:11:46.760 --> 00:11:49.610
A lot of people know me and trust me.

00:11:49.610 --> 00:11:53.090
And so I said, "I wanna
create a media company"

00:11:53.090 --> 00:11:53.923
and I did it.

00:11:53.923 --> 00:11:55.250
I'm doing it with my husband, John.

00:11:55.250 --> 00:11:56.680
He's the CEO,

00:11:56.680 --> 00:11:58.680
'cause I'm sort of more
the creative person.

00:11:58.680 --> 00:12:00.362
He's a very strong, smart-
- Business minded, yeah.

00:12:00.362 --> 00:12:01.480
- Businessman.

00:12:01.480 --> 00:12:04.282
And so we have a
newsletter, "Wake Up Call,"

00:12:04.282 --> 00:12:05.900
which, you know,

00:12:05.900 --> 00:12:09.170
basically gives all the
news you need to know

00:12:09.170 --> 00:12:10.003
and helps,

00:12:10.003 --> 00:12:12.610
we have medical information,
health and wellness,

00:12:12.610 --> 00:12:15.184
lifestyle, fashion, all
sorts of different things

00:12:15.184 --> 00:12:18.590
in just one digestible
email every morning,

00:12:18.590 --> 00:12:19.830
called "Wake Up Call."

00:12:19.830 --> 00:12:23.510
Then I have a podcast that I've
been doing for a few years.

00:12:23.510 --> 00:12:25.280
I'm developing some documentaries

00:12:25.280 --> 00:12:26.250
and scripted projects.

00:12:26.250 --> 00:12:27.310
So basically-
- Wow.

00:12:27.310 --> 00:12:29.270
- We're doing so many
things at the company,

00:12:29.270 --> 00:12:30.520
it's just really exciting.

00:12:30.520 --> 00:12:31.353
- It's fun you get

00:12:31.353 --> 00:12:32.320
to do with him too, yeah.
- It's great to be the boss

00:12:32.320 --> 00:12:33.940
of you, isn't it?

00:12:33.940 --> 00:12:35.350
♪ Amen ♪

00:12:35.350 --> 00:12:36.650
- Yes!
(audience applauding)

00:12:36.650 --> 00:12:38.080
- Right?
- Yes.

00:12:38.080 --> 00:12:38.913
It is.

00:12:38.913 --> 00:12:40.580
It's cool that you get to
work with your husband too

00:12:40.580 --> 00:12:41.413
while doing it.
- Yeah.

00:12:41.413 --> 00:12:42.246
- That's really cool.

00:12:42.246 --> 00:12:43.250
That y'all get to do something
so beautiful together.

00:12:43.250 --> 00:12:44.857
- Yeah. We haven't killed each other yet.

00:12:44.857 --> 00:12:46.007
So that's positive.
(Kelly laughing)

00:12:46.007 --> 00:12:49.720
We have a lot of, you know, young people

00:12:49.720 --> 00:12:52.610
